# 10. New Radio UI and Data Requirements

These are explicit new requirements and have not yet been verified as implemented.

## 10.1 Remove Capacity Health

Capacity Health is not wanted.

Remove it from the web UI and Excel.

Inspect the backend for a dedicated Capacity Health status calculation and remove it if no other required check depends on it.

Preserve unrelated checks.

## 10.2 One unified Links table

The user does not want separate presentation sections for each link type.

All supported link types must appear in a unified table:

```text
RTN
EBand
NRay
Intracom
```

Use the title:

```text
Links
```

Apply the title consistently to the web dashboard and Excel report.

Do not discard link-type metadata from the underlying records. The user is requesting one combined presentation, not removal of the individual type identities.

## 10.3 Numeric values instead of status words

The requested primary radio table is:

| Radio Host | RX/TX Difference | Modulation |
|---|---:|---:|
| Real radio hostname | Real numeric difference | Real numeric modulation |

The numeric cells must display the actual measured values.

For example, a value such as `3.2` must be displayed as `3.2`, even if the associated status is Not OK because of an independently verified condition.

Do not substitute a health word for the measured value.

Do not fabricate numbers when data is missing.

Keep numeric measurements separate from the status evaluation.

The user has explicitly asked for these three columns as the primary display:

```text
Radio Host
RX/TX Difference
Modulation
```

Any extra columns should be considered only if they are genuinely necessary for the user’s requirements.

## 10.4 Light or milky background

The existing frontend uses a dark-blue theme.

The user wants a light, milky/cream background for the radio table and its numeric cells instead of dark blue.

Change the relevant CSS without unnecessarily redesigning unrelated areas.

Use appropriate contrast and readable text.

## 10.5 Status colors

Required color semantics:

```text
OK        → GREEN
NOT OK    → RED
UNKNOWN   → GRAY
```

Numeric values must remain visible.

Unknown must not be treated as zero.

Missing, stale, or unavailable data must not falsely appear healthy.

## 10.6 Twelve-hour historical persistence

The user explicitly requires checking the last twelve hours to determine whether the same problem has persisted.

The system must distinguish:

1. A current healthy condition.
2. A current problem that appears newly.
3. A problem that demonstrably persists over the relevant twelve-hour period.
4. An unknown condition caused by missing/inadequate data.

The exact wording/status to use for persistent problems should be practical for a NOC operator, and the UI should make clear that the problem has been present for a while.

Use real Zabbix history and trigger/event data.

Do not infer continuous persistence from a single recent failing sample.

Do not infer recovery or health from missing data.

A trigger that occurred once in the last twelve hours does not automatically prove it remained active for twelve hours.

Check timestamps and recovery events where available.

If the historical evidence is insufficient, show the appropriate Unknown/insufficient-history indication.

The current project includes an existing six-hour radio analysis configuration and a seven-day history configuration. The new user requirement explicitly specifies twelve-hour persistence and must not be silently reduced to the six-hour window.

Preserve all existing valid link-specific logic while implementing this feature.

