# 11. Orchestrator

Files:

```text
/opt/pop-audit/orchestrator/app/main.py
/opt/pop-audit/orchestrator/app/runner.py
/opt/pop-audit/orchestrator/app/models.py
```

Port:

```text
8001
```

The Orchestrator has implemented asynchronous audit jobs.

The lifecycle includes:

```text
QUEUED
DISCOVERY
RUNNING
COMPLETED
FAILED
```

The main endpoint is:

```text
POST /audit
```

It returns a job identifier and starts an asynchronous audit.

Job retrieval:

```text
GET /audit/{job_id}
```

The Orchestrator calls the appropriate microservices to perform the actual checks.

It aggregates results into an overall audit status.

Status priority:

```text
CRITICAL
WARNING
UNKNOWN
NOT_AVAILABLE
PASS
```

The status extraction logic considers a wrapper result and the health status inside its `data` object.

## Important limitation

The current implementation stores jobs in an in-memory dictionary:

```python
self.jobs = {}
```

This means that a restart of the Orchestrator process can lose active and completed job records.

Persistent job storage is a production-hardening requirement.

Inspect the existing database environment variables and `/opt/pop-audit/sql/` before designing persistence.

There are existing PostgreSQL configuration values, but do not assume the application already has a working job-history schema.

Do not change or destroy existing database objects.

Design any new persistence work so that it does not interfere with unrelated PostgreSQL workloads.

## Report integration

The Orchestrator was modified so that after a successful audit reaches `COMPLETED`, it automatically calls the Report Service.

The intended behavior is:

```text
Audit completed
      ↓
Generate Excel
      ↓
Store report metadata in job
```

Report-generation failure is designed to be nonfatal to the completed audit.

Verify the current source before assuming this behavior still works.

